# Registry tables and bulk actions

Help people find the correct records, understand what is selected and predict the scope of a bulk operation. Entities, devices, areas, labels and categories are related but not interchangeable. Sorting or filtering must not silently broaden a selection.

## Scope and native ownership

Native organisation features distinguish areas, floors, labels, categories and groups. Registry screens own their selection, sorting and saved-view behaviour. The local Publishing finder is a fixed-record demonstration, not a replacement registry or permission system. [Official Home Assistant source](https://www.home-assistant.io/docs/organizing/), reviewed 11 November 2026.

This page completes the design specification for **HA-GAP-029**. Its examples are synthetic documentation, not a claim that a new native feature or adapter has been installed. The application integration must meet the task-specific acceptance below.

## States and content decisions

| Source condition | Required presentation | Boundary |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Loaded records | Show the current filter/sort context and meaningful names. | Do not conflate an entity row with a whole device. |
| Selected rows | State the selected count and whether hidden filtered rows remain selected. | Do not infer Select all means every record on the server. |
| No filter matches | Explain the active criteria and a real reset route. | Do not claim the registry has no entities. |
| Unavailable / restricted data | Name the actual retrieval or access limitation. | Do not describe a failed response as an empty result. |
| Partial bulk outcome | Show successful, failed and unconfirmed members separately. | Do not announce All updated while any result is missing. |

## Integration contract

1. Use native stable record IDs and the server’s authorized data. Keep display names separate from copyable entity identifiers.
2. Before a bulk operation, resolve its exact target set and present a count plus meaningful review route. Retain native selection semantics through filter, sort and page changes; document them rather than guessing.
3. Only expose operations supported by every target or make per-target restrictions explicit. An area assignment, label change, disable operation and removal have different consequences.
4. Saved filters or views require the actual native persistence contract and scope. A locally filtered documentation table must never claim it saved an account preference.

## Accessible interaction

- Use real table headers or the native equivalent, visible sorting state and named row selection controls. Keyboard access must not require clicking an entire interactive row.
- Announce changed result/selection counts without announcing the full table after each keystroke.
- Keep row actions reachable at narrow widths and enlarged text. Provide a named way to review selected rows before a destructive operation.

## Failure and recovery

- If a selected row disappears or permission changes, revalidate the target set before execution and explain the change.
- For partial failure, preserve successful results and expose only supported retry scopes; do not blindly repeat completed destructive work.
- If the registry cannot load, retain filter inputs as supported but do not display previous data as a fresh authorized response.

